A Geometric Bifurcation Theory of Megafire Emergence

Toward a Predictive, Information‑Theoretic, and Control‑Theoretic Framework

Ouadi Maakoul


Abstract

Megafires are catastrophic wildfire events that overwhelm all suppression resources. Their onset is a nonlinear regime transition poorly captured by empirical models. This dissertation constructs a self‑contained mathematical theory that identifies the precise dynamical threshold separating local, controllable fires from self‑sustaining, uncontainable megafires. From the global energy balance of a reaction–diffusion–advection system a dimensionless Critical Fire Number R_f is derived, expressing the ratio of combustion heat release to total cooling power. In one space dimension a rigorous bifurcation analysis proves that travelling combustion waves appear through a saddle‑node bifurcation at a universal critical value R_f^*: below the threshold all ignitions extinguish; above it a stable propagating front—the megafire state—emerges. Equipping the parameter space with a Fisher information metric shows that the condition number of the metric diverges as the critical manifold is approached, providing a model‑based early‑warning signal that requires no empirical tuning. A control‑theoretic formulation using Control Lyapunov–Barrier Functions establishes that, in one dimension, a safe, stabilising suppression policy exists if and only if R_f < R_f^*; this yields the first mathematically precise definition of a megafire as a fire that has crossed the boundary of controllability. The extension to two and three dimensions is stated as a conjecture supported by heuristic arguments. Finally, a Bayesian data‑assimilation architecture based on the Ensemble Score Filter and physics‑informed neural network emulators is specified; it estimates R_f in real time and computes the posterior probability that the landscape has entered the megafire regime. The entire work is theoretical: every claim is explicitly labelled as a Definition, Lemma, Proposition, or Conjecture, all proofs are provided or rigorously sketched, and no numerical experiments or physical tests are performed. The framework is intended as a rigorous mathematical foundation for future predictive systems, operational early‑warning tools, and adaptive land‑management policies.

