Chapter 2. Mathematical Preliminaries

Let \Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d (d=1,2) be a bounded C^2 domain. Standard Lebesgue and Sobolev spaces are used; \|\cdot\| denotes the L^2 norm.

Definition 1 (Classical solution). A pair (T,Y) is a classical solution of a parabolic system on [0,\tau] if T,Y\in C^{2,1}(\Omega\times(0,\tau))\cap C(\overline{\Omega}\times[0,\tau]) and the equations hold pointwise. Smooth nonlinearities guarantee local classical solutions.

Lemma 1 (Maximum principle). For \partial_t T-\alpha\Delta T+\mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T+cT\le 0 with Neumann conditions and T(0)\le 0, we have T(t)\le 0. Analogously, 0\le Y(t)\le 1.

Bifurcation Theory. A saddle‑node bifurcation of equilibria occurs when an eigenvalue of the linearisation crosses zero transversely; the normal form is \dot x = r + x^2. In infinite dimensions the same structure persists on a one‑dimensional centre manifold.

Information Geometry. For a parametric family of probability densities p(\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta}), the Fisher information metric is

g_{ij}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \mathbb{E}\big[ \partial_{\theta_i}\log p\; \partial_{\theta_j}\log p \big].
\]  

For a Gaussian likelihood with mean \mathcal{F}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) and variance \sigma^2 I, this reduces to g = \sigma^{-2} J^{\mathsf T}J, where J is the Jacobian of \mathcal{F}.

Control Lyapunov–Barrier Functions (CLBF). For a control‑affine system \dot x = f(x)+g(x)u and a safe set \mathcal{C}, a function B:\mathcal{C}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is a CLBF if (i) B(x)\to\infty as x\to\partial\mathcal{C}, and (ii) there exists a bounded control u=k(x) and a class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha such that \dot B + \alpha(B)\le 0 on \mathcal{C}.

