Chapter 6. Control‑Theoretic Framework

6.1 Controlled PDE and Safe Set.
Suppression is modelled as a distributed cooling rate \mu(x,t)\in[0,\mu_{\max}] added to the temperature equation:

\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}\partial_x^2 T - w\partial_x T - \mathrm{St}\,T + \lambda\Psi(T)Y - \mu(x,t)T. \tag{7}

In one dimension the safe set is

\mathcal{C} = \Big\{ (T,Y)\in C(\overline{\Omega})\times L^2(\Omega): \int_\Omega Y\,dx \ge Y_{\text{safe}},\; \sup_{x\in\Omega} T(x) \le T_{\text{safe}} \Big\}.

6.2 Containable Regime (R_f<R_f^*).

Proposition 4 (Safe stabilisation, formal construction). If R_f<R_f^*, the unburnt equilibrium is asymptotically stable. There exists a high‑gain linear feedback \mu = k_p T and a CLBF B(T,Y) such that the closed‑loop system satisfies \dot B + \alpha(B)\le 0 on \mathcal{C}.
Proof sketch. Linearising (7) about (0,1) gives \partial_t T = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}\partial_x^2 T - w\partial_x T - (\mathrm{St} - \lambda\gamma) T - k_p T, with \gamma = \Psi'(0)>0. When R_f<1 we have \eta = \mathrm{St}-\lambda\gamma >0. The Lyapunov functional V = \frac12\int T^2 dx satisfies \dot V \le -(\eta+k_p)\|T\|^2. One then adds a barrier function \Phi (e.g., -\log(\text{distance to } \partial\mathcal{C})) and chooses k_p and a small weight \nu so that B = V + \nu\Phi is a CLBF. A fully explicit verification requires constructing \Phi and bounding the nonlinear terms; the argument here is a formal outline.

6.3 Uncontainability for R_f>R_f^*.

Proposition 5 (Uncontainability in 1D). If R_f>R_f^*, then for any admissible control \mu\le\mu_{\max} there exist initial conditions in \mathcal{C} such that the solution leaves \mathcal{C} in finite time. No CLBF exists.
Proof. By Proposition 2 there exists a stable travelling wave (\Theta_*,\Upsilon_*) with speed c>0. Embed a segment of this wave as initial condition, with T\le T_{\text{safe}} and \int Y\,dx > Y_{\text{safe}}. The maximal control \mu\equiv\mu_{\max} yields a supersolution with reduced \lambda' that still propagates. By the comparison principle, any actual control leads to fuel consumption and eventual escape from \mathcal{C}.

Conjecture 2 (2D/3D uncontainability). For d\ge 2, when R_f>R_f^*, no bounded control can keep the system within a reasonable safe set for all initial conditions, because stable planar travelling waves propagate regardless of bounded cooling.

