Appendix A. Nondimensionalisation and Derivation of R_f

From the dimensional equations, scaling time by L/U and temperature by \Delta T_c yields (2) with the given dimensionless numbers. Integrating the steady‑state temperature equation over a control volume V enclosing the front and applying the divergence theorem gives

\int_{\partial V}\!\Big(\frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}\nabla T - \mathbf{w}T\Big)\!\cdot\!\mathbf{n}\,dS - \mathrm{St}\int_V T\,d\mathbf{x} + \lambda\int_V \Psi(T)Y\,d\mathbf{x} = 0.
\]  

Lemma A1 (Scaling of boundary flux). Assume the front width is order one in dimensionless units. Then the boundary flux term scales as \mathrm{Pe}^{-1}\int_V T\,d\mathbf{x}. Hence the total cooling power is proportional to (\mathrm{St}+\mathrm{Pe}^{-1})\int_V T\,d\mathbf{x}, and balancing with the heat release gives the critical condition \lambda \sim \mathrm{St}+\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}, motivating R_f = \lambda/(\mathrm{St}+\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}).

---

Appendix B. 1D Travelling Wave Analysis (Formal Asymptotics)

The flame‑sheet limit \varepsilon\to0 fixes the reaction at \Theta=1. For w=0, the linear ODEs ahead and behind give \Theta(\xi) = e^{\mu_+\xi} for \xi<0, e^{\mu_-\xi} for \xi>0, with \mu_{\pm} = (\mathrm{Pe}/2)(-c\pm\sqrt{c^2+4\mathrm{St}/\mathrm{Pe}}). Matching the jump in \Theta' yields (5). For small \varepsilon, expanding the reaction zone structure gives \lambda(c;\varepsilon) = \lambda_0(c)[1 - \varepsilon\,\mathcal{K}(c) + O(\varepsilon^2)]. The function \mathcal{K}(c) is positive and has non‑zero derivative; a complete inner/outer expansion (not carried out here) would confirm the non‑degeneracy of the turning point. The existence of a fold follows from the non‑monotonicity introduced by the correction.

---

Appendix C. CLBF Construction (Formal Sketch)

We outline the construction referenced in Proposition 4. For the linearised system with \mu=k_p T, define V = \frac12\int T^2 dx and observe \dot V \le -(\eta+k_p)\|T\|^2. Choose a barrier function \Phi(Y,\|T\|_\infty) that is smooth, non‑negative, and tends to +\infty as either \|T\|_\infty\to T_{\text{safe}}^- or \int Y\,dx \to Y_{\text{safe}}^+. Set B = V + \nu\Phi. To ensure \dot B + \alpha(B)\le 0, one must bound the growth of \Phi along trajectories using the fact that the reaction term is globally bounded on \mathcal{C} and that the high‑gain feedback dominates. A fully rigorous verification would require explicit expressions for \Phi and quantitative estimates relating k_p, \nu, and the Lipschitz constant of \Psi; this remains an open technical task.

